data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":"The internet is often imagined as an abstract, invisible cloud, but it is actually a massive","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":", highly structured physical infrastructure. At its core, the internet is a global network of computers connected to share information. To","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" understand how it works, we can trace the journey of data from the physical cables buried beneath our feet to the high-level","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" protocols that display websites on our screens. This journey spans several layers, each building on the last.\n\n### 1. The Physical Layer:","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" Cables, Light, and Radio Waves\nThe foundation of the internet is entirely physical. When you send an email, stream a","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" video, or load a webpage, your data begins as binary code—a sequence of 1s and 0s. To","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" travel, this digital data must be converted into physical signals. \n\nFor wired connections, these signals are transmitted as pulses of light traveling through","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" fiber-optic cables, or as electrical currents moving through copper telephone and coaxial lines. Underneath the world's oceans lies","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" a massive network of submarine fiber-optic cables that connect continents, allowing data to travel across the globe at nearly the speed of light","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":". For wireless connections, such as Wi-Fi or cellular data, the 1s and 0s are converted into radio waves.","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" These waves travel through the air to a local router or cell tower, which quickly translates them back into electrical or light signals to","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" join the wired physical network.\n\n### 2. The Network Layer: IP Addresses and Routing\nOnce data is converted into physical signals,","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" it needs a way to find its destination. This is the job of the Network Layer, governed primarily by the Internet Protocol (IP","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":"). \n\nEvery device connected to the internet—whether it is your smartphone, a smart fridge, or a massive server owned","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" by Google—is assigned a unique identifier called an IP address (such as `192.168.","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":"1.1` or a longer IPv6 address). \n\nTo travel efficiently, large files are not sent all at once. Instead, they","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" are broken down into smaller chunks called \"packets.\" Each packet contains a portion of the data, along with a \"header\"","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" that lists the sender’s IP address and the destination's IP address. Specialized computers called routers act as traffic controllers","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" across the global network. They read the destination IP on each packet and forward it along the most efficient path. Because network traffic","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" constantly fluctuates, packets from the same file may take entirely different routes across the globe, arriving at the destination at slightly different times and","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" out of order.\n\n### 3. The Transport Layer: TCP and UDP\nThis is where the Transport Layer comes","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" in, dominated by the Transmission Control Protocol (TCP). TCP sits on top of IP (often referred to together as TCP/","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":"IP) and ensures that data is delivered reliably. \n\nBefore any data is sent, TCP establishes a connection between the sender and","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" receiver using a process called a \"three-way handshake.\" Once the connection is active, TCP numbers the packets. When the packets arrive at the","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" destination, TCP reassembles them in the correct order. If any packets are lost, delayed, or corrupted during transit,","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" TCP detects the error and requests that the sender retransmit those specific packets. \n\nFor real-time applications where speed is","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" more important than perfect accuracy—such as live video streaming, online gaming, or voice calls—a different protocol called UDP","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" (User Datagram Protocol) is used. UDP sends packets continuously without checking if they arrived, prioritizing speed over reliability.\n\n### 4.","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" The Domain Name System (DNS): The Internet's Phonebook\nWhile computers communicate using numerical IP addresses, humans prefer","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" easy-to-remember names like `wikipedia.org`. The Domain Name System (DNS) bridges this gap. \n\nWhen","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" you type a website address into your browser, your computer does not immediately know where to go. It first sends a quick","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" query to a DNS server. The DNS server looks up the domain name in its database and returns the corresponding IP address of","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" the web server hosting that site. Once your browser receives this IP address, it can initiate the TCP/IP connection to request the website'","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":"s content.\n\n### 5. The Application Layer: HTTP and HTTPS\nWith the physical connection established, routing determined","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":", delivery guaranteed by TCP, and the IP address resolved by DNS, the Application Layer can finally do its job. For","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" web browsing, this layer is governed by the Hypertext Transfer Protocol (HTTP).\n\nYour browser sends an HTTP \"GET\" request to the target","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" web server, essentially asking, \"Please send me the files for this webpage.\" The server processes this request and sends back an HTTP response.","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" This response includes a status code (such as `200 OK` if successful, or the famous `404 Not Found","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":"` if the page doesn't exist) along with the website's HTML, CSS, JavaScript, and image files. Your browser then","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" reads these files and renders them into the visual webpage you see.\n\nToday, almost all web traffic uses HTTPS (the","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" \"S\" standing for Secure). HTTPS encrypts the HTTP data using Transport Layer Security (TLS), ensuring that any data","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" exchanged between your browser and the server cannot be intercepted or tampered with by third parties.\n\n### Conclusion\nIn a fraction of a second,","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" this entire sequence occurs. When you click a link, your request is translated into light, routed across continents via undersea cables, managed by IP","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":", verified by TCP, translated by DNS, and delivered via HTTP. It is a monumental feat of global engineering, operating","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" continuously and seamlessly in the background of our daily lives.","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

